{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1465319413,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDIyNDM0ODQ3NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/506#issuecomment-224348475"},"message":"Yaroslav Halchenko wrote:\n\n\u003e @joeyh -- what do you think would be the best resolution?\n\nIt's worth considering whether git-annex should be passing these \nGIT_DIR etc environments to the special remote process. \n\nWell, if git-annex didn't set these, and git was run with --git-dir or\n--work-tree, it would not communicate those overrides on to the special\nremote process, and so if the special remote then ran a git command\n(including re-running git-annex), it would run with the wrong values.\n\nAlso, git --git-dir annex ... sets GIT_DIR to communicate it to\ngit-annex, so it's consistent for git-annex to pass these environment\nvariables on to its children as well. (git-annex goes ahead and always\nsets GIT_DIR and GIT_WORK_TREE even when --git-dir and --work-tree are\nnot set, for reasons of consistency/simplicity.)\n\nPerhaps git-annex should set GIT_DIR etc to absolute paths in case\nthe special remote process chdir's. However, the relative paths are\nuseful especially on Windows with its very short limits on the length of\npaths.\n\n\u003e 1. sanitize the environment (GIT_DIR GIT_WORK_TREE, anything else?) before\n\u003e    invoking git/annex from top directory\n\nIf you mean sanitize by clearing then no; it would break --git-dir and\n--work-tree setting propigation.\n\nIf you're going to chdir, it makes sense to adjust these relative paths\nto be absolute or relative to the new CWD.\n\n## \n\nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1465520793,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50Njg4MDIwNzcx"},"status":2}]}